A||||4||fierospeeder||2.3L swap||||1
Z||000000||fierospeeder||11-24-1999||01:40 AM||||I own a tbird tc also which has a 2.3L turbo engine.  Is it possible to put one of these in a fiero.  I thought about finding a case and building ground up with a bigger turbo and forged pistons.  They are capable to making 400 hp.  stock tbird makes 190hp.  at 8psi.  stock turbo can be set at 17psi which most of the guys who own this engine run at. would this be a major project?  I can find a computer from a scrap yard... is the motor and trans hookup the problem?<BR>otherwise i thought about just doing the 2.8 swap instead.||||reg||1
Z||000001||WKDFIRO||11-24-1999||10:13 AM||||Granted that the installation of a Ford product into the back of a Fiero would be interesting it would also be, even to some experienced mechanics, a nightmare. Fabrication of the adapter plates to mount it on to the tranny would be a piece of cake as well as any modifications of brackets etc when you are taking a RWD powertrain and putting it sideways and trying to keep the turbo from frying a fuel line. The real fun begins with the wiring. It would be doubtful a Ford ECM would be compatable with a GM speedometer or tach, temp, or maybe even the fuel gauge. <BR>In California it would be close to impossible with smog regulations. Go V6. Or if you want the 400 hp, go all the way to V8. Just as much work but a little more straight forward.||||reg||4
Z||000002||88 Fiero||11-24-1999||10:17 AM||||I read an article back in the eighties...where someone hook up a Ford Taurus engine that have the 24valve Yamaha made in his Fiero...It was a wicked car....||||reg||1
Z||000003||DJRice||11-24-1999||04:11 PM||||Why not just strip it of its turbocharger?<P>But ultimately you would be better off if you found some type of GM Donor engine.||||reg||1
Z||000004||theogre||11-24-1999||11:53 PM||||I've worked on the Ford 2.3l engine block allot. <P>The ford turbo 2.3 is esentially the same motor as originally used in Pinto/Bobcat with the turbo and EFI pretty much slapped on. The turbo was not a well thought out addition. The nonturbo version of these motors worked well. I just scraped one at over 100,000 miles on it. Even with a flat #1 I drove it at 55+ to the salvage yard.<P>The turbo version of the motor is known to have cam problems caused by oil stavation. If you do this make very bloody sure the OHC and everything else in the top is prime.<P>Could you put this in a Fiero? not without a horrid amount of work. For one thing it's not a transverse engine design. There are no transmissions I know of that would make it so. You would probably have to adapt it to run on a TH125. You won't likely get a clutch ot starter to fit it onto a stick. Ford Starters bolt to the bell houseing thru the shield. They don't bolt to the block and there is no area to adapt a GM starter to do so. Ford starters are nearly all on the right side. You probly would have to make a custom head shell for the starter to work on the left You cannot rotate the head shell, it carries the bendix engage solinoid.<P>If you are going to consider a hybrid setup, Gen 1 Dodge Intrepid has a hell of a strong V6 setup available in it. Those a FWD. You could take the whole engine tranny and electrical. Your hard part is figuring out what half shafts to use or making a pair up. (there are only a small number of half shaft ends. you probly would need to put chryslers inner with GMs outer end on a custom made shaft. Sounds harder than it is. Any machine shop could make a new shaft with the right splining and length.) <P>Would it be worth it to use the intrepid settup? considering intrepid is considerably heavier and has a top <B>cruising</B> speed in excess of 100mph..... Yes I said cruising.... I had one on the NY thruway once at over 105 for an hour straight. It wasn't even breathing hard yet. I only slowed down to get of in Phoughkeepsee. That beast held 105+ up hill BTW. I still had lots of gas peddal left. <P>If you try holding one of GM's 2.8's at that speed for long without doing major prep to the motor it's likely to not last long. Won't likely blow apart, but it will be hurting. GM's stock motors even now aren't built to last well. I've already seen allot of the new TA's and camaro smoking. About half the 2.8's I see in Fiero or cavalier are smoking full time. If you look at a 2.8, make sure it's in a car that can be started. You don't want one if it does anything more than a small puff just as it starts.<p>[This message has been edited by theogre (edited 11-25-1999).]||||reg||1
